WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:09.120
- So I've titled this Control
= Prediction + Engineering.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:10.830
I think this is the grammar that I use

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:12.900
to think about these sorts of questions.

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:15.570
I have an amazing post-doctoral
associate in my lab

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:17.047
who's here, who I sat down and he said,

00:00:17.047 --> 00:00:19.710
"I didn't know you worked
on causal inference."

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:21.367
And I said, "Well, you know,

00:00:21.367 --> 00:00:23.827
"it looks like we have a lot
to know about each other,

00:00:23.827 --> 00:00:25.057
"but I think we're gonna use the grammar

00:00:25.057 --> 00:00:27.507
"of kind of prediction,
which is largely what we use

00:00:27.507 --> 00:00:29.947
"in the evolutionary and
bioengineering paradigms,

00:00:29.947 --> 00:00:31.207
"which is the ones in which,

00:00:31.207 --> 00:00:33.420
"the ones in which I operate today."

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:35.520
Before doing anything else, really,

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:38.880
I wanna acknowledge the
organizers of this meeting,

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:40.710
everybody who supported this.

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:41.850
This is one of the coolest

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:43.860
and most interesting
gatherings I've been at

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:45.690
in my several years at Yale.

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:47.610
I always say that this
institution needs more

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:49.860
kind of renegade, nerd gatherings.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:52.740
There's not enough, and this
absolutely qualifies as one.

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:54.780
So I appreciate everyone who supported,

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:56.460
and all of you, of course, for attending,

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:59.160
all of the individuals
in my research program,

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:01.080
all the individuals
who've supported this work

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:02.620
in various ways through the years.

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:05.790
And of course, a large
library of mentors, mentees,

00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:07.590
former mentees who are now mentors,

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:09.840
people who now support me in various ways.

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:11.280
They've influenced this work

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:14.160
in various ways through the years.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:16.590
And my newest family,
the Santa Fe Institute,

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:17.680
which has really been important

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:21.060
for integrating this dimension
of my research program,

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:24.300
and further my identity
for asking questions

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.160
that are related to what
we're discussing here

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:27.690
at this workshop today.

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:29.940
So I want to make sure
they are acknowledged.

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:32.520
You know, the reason
why I titled the talk,

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:34.050
my title came from some popular writing.

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:36.360
I was actually writing
the article on the top

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:38.400
where I actually introduced this,

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:39.960
really just a conceptual framework

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:42.360
for thinking about how we're thinking

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:46.140
about controlling evolutionary
biology in various settings,

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:49.260
largely in the construction
of biomolecules.

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:51.270
And I came up with this framework,

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:55.170
control equals prediction
plus engineering.

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:56.760
It's really just a, like I said,

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:59.180
it's a conceptual anchor
for how to think about

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:01.920
how we're, the types
of information we need

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:05.400
to be able to control a
complex biological system.

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:06.900
Right, and I kind of use this as an anchor

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:08.280
for the rest of this talk,

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:09.480
but mostly we're gonna be focusing

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:11.670
on the prediction component of this

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:13.920
since that's kind of the one related,

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:17.010
not the same as, but related to causality.

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:18.630
How do I enter these problems?

00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:20.790
Well, I mean, I think my
basic science program,

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:22.590
I've been described as a
lot of different things.

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:24.240
I give a talk at many different sorts

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:25.950
of scientific audiences.

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:29.190
So depending on who I'm around
is the way I'm described,

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:31.320
but a computational biologist mostly,

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:34.170
and we use a range of
methods where we use disease

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:35.640
to ask fundamental questions

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:37.770
kind of about the way nature works.

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:39.000
And I think on the flip side,

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.460
we use kind of theory in various paradigms

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:43.350
to potentially improve how we think

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:45.747
about disease and epidemics.

00:02:45.747 --> 00:02:47.160
But I think there's another component

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:48.090
of my research program,

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:50.580
which is going to be very,
very active here today.

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:53.880
And I think it is an act really
in all areas of my program.

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:56.010
And that's kind of the intersection

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:57.840
between data and culture.

00:02:57.840 --> 00:02:59.580
I think this is something that began

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:02.340
as something that I
was doing just because,

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:04.560
but this has actually become
a real technical paradigm

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:05.430
for my work.

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:07.950
And I think it dances very intimately

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:10.260
with some of the other
dimensions of my work.

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:12.450
So you'll be introduced to this as well.

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:13.860
But I think really
there's been a combination

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:16.410
between these identities recently.

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:17.243
Like I said, which is,

00:03:17.243 --> 00:03:18.820
and I think really where I land is that

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:21.750
I focus on how interactions
shape constraints

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:23.400
in biological systems.

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:25.470
Whether we're talking
about engineered systems

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:26.910
or we're talking about artificial systems

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:28.470
or informational systems.

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:30.690
I mean, really you can define,

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:31.590
we can define all of them

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:33.060
by what are the guardrails around them?

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:34.460
What are the things that
are actually shaping

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:37.020
how those systems are constructed?

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:39.120
In my sense, the way things evolve,

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:42.573
these interactions shape
molecular evolution.

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:46.620
They constrain how epidemics manifest

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:48.270
and they constrain science in the world,

00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:49.980
which is an all in which it is embedded.

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:51.390
So this is really how I can frame,

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:53.130
I call myself an interaction nerd.

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:54.390
I like when I put things together

00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:55.520
and I get something surprising

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:57.680
and I kind of use biological systems

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:01.530
to be able to ask those
sorts of questions.

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:04.380
Now, when we think about
what a constraint is,

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:07.320
I think this manifests in
all the talks that we heard.

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:08.520
We heard this outstanding,

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:10.827
just this morning we've heard
about different constraints

00:04:10.827 --> 00:04:13.410
and the way they shape
the systems of interest.

00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:16.140
Be they cosmological,
be they anthropological,

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:18.120
be they cellular, be they biophysical.

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:19.200
We can think about a lot of ways.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:21.120
And so this is like one
dimension of constraint

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:22.290
where I mostly live.

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:25.590
I study proteins and I study
kind of protein folding.

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:27.360
And here you have an enzyme.

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:29.610
Dihydrofolate reductase,

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:31.650
basically it catalyzes a
reaction at different rates

00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:32.580
and different temperatures.

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:34.230
So in this situation.

00:04:34.230 --> 00:04:35.670
Temperature is a context,

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:38.610
which is kind of constraining
what this enzyme can do.

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:41.340
And in my lab, we identify kind
of the way this constrains,

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:42.300
the way it evolves.

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:43.410
It sets the expectations

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:45.690
for the way this sort of protein evolves.

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:47.100
We can think about constraint this way.

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:49.950
If many of you work on the kind
of ecosystem ecology scale,

00:04:49.950 --> 00:04:51.690
or if you think about climate change,

00:04:51.690 --> 00:04:53.520
or you think about species distribution,

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:54.353
or any kind of really,

00:04:54.353 --> 00:04:56.910
even this is in many ways geopolitical.

00:04:56.910 --> 00:04:58.350
All right, you can think about forces

00:04:58.350 --> 00:05:00.900
operating on this level.

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:04.200
Some of us kind of live in
this level here, me less so.

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:06.120
This is, any New Yorkers in the audience?

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:07.230
This is the Queensbridge.

00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:08.063
Okay, good.

00:05:08.063 --> 00:05:09.510
This is the Queensbridge houses.

00:05:09.510 --> 00:05:11.520
So this is the largest
public housing complex

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:12.840
in the United States now,

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:15.000
and it's home to all my
heroes from growing up.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:16.087
And I think one of the great question is,

00:05:16.087 --> 00:05:18.600
you know, what is it about
neighborhoods like this

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:21.000
that give birth to so many
people gifted with words

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:23.250
in terms of hip hop artists.

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:24.990
And this is something that was
very, very important to me.

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:26.610
We think about this in a lot of contexts.

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:27.600
What is it about this,

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:29.180
these sets of constraints and contexts

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:31.170
that kind of created that opportunity

00:05:31.170 --> 00:05:34.590
for that sort of linguistic advancement?

00:05:34.590 --> 00:05:35.940
But then of course, this one.

00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:37.350
These constraints are tied together.

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:39.330
It's not different than
when we heard about scales.

00:05:39.330 --> 00:05:41.490
I think there's resonance
between these things.

00:05:41.490 --> 00:05:43.800
There's a relationship between
some of those constraints

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:46.320
we talked about, for example,
like that constraint,

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:47.520
that relates to that constraint.

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:48.810
But this constraint, of course,

00:05:48.810 --> 00:05:49.860
in this context, of course,

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:51.690
did not grow out of the ground.

00:05:51.690 --> 00:05:53.430
This one came out because
of constraints like this,

00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:54.960
historical constraints.

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:57.630
Like they were active
decisions made by homo sapiens

00:05:57.630 --> 00:05:58.590
about who could live where,

00:05:58.590 --> 00:06:00.900
who could be how you treat certain people

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:02.130
and what have you.

00:06:02.130 --> 00:06:04.350
So the point is that all
areas of my research program

00:06:04.350 --> 00:06:06.960
and all areas of all the
things we're discussing here

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:09.600
in this, right, with regation
to causation and inference,

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:13.200
they're being shaped by various forces.

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:15.000
And so what I'm gonna
argue is that, right,

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:16.290
the success, if you will,

00:06:16.290 --> 00:06:18.480
or the effectiveness of causal inference

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:21.000
and, or in my case, prediction or control

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:23.370
is really gonna be grounded by, right,

00:06:23.370 --> 00:06:25.980
our understanding of the way
these sorts of constraints

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:28.170
influence our systems of interest,

00:06:28.170 --> 00:06:29.370
our understanding of them,

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:31.440
our ability to parameterize them.

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:32.970
And that really is, right,

00:06:32.970 --> 00:06:33.900
that's kind of the framework

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:36.000
that I approach these
sorts of questions with.

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:38.340
And so we'll meet various
components of these

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:39.173
and various actors.

00:06:39.173 --> 00:06:41.517
Now, I'm not nearly as
smart enough to study this

00:06:41.517 --> 00:06:43.000
and this well enough.

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:45.060
There's people who have
studied this for centuries,

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:46.980
infinitely better than I ever could.

00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:50.970
But I have found the
attempt to do so, right,

00:06:50.970 --> 00:06:53.010
to them to be the idiot
in the room, right,

00:06:53.010 --> 00:06:54.330
when we're reading about
that sort of thing,

00:06:54.330 --> 00:06:55.163
to be refreshing,

00:06:55.163 --> 00:06:59.610
and it has added to the dimensions
of my work in many ways.

00:06:59.610 --> 00:07:01.710
So, like I said, we're
gonna get through a lot.

00:07:01.710 --> 00:07:03.120
So we're doing okay?

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:03.953
All right.

00:07:03.953 --> 00:07:05.730
So control prediction is engineering.

00:07:05.730 --> 00:07:06.780
So again, like I said, right,

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:07.980
these sorts of constraints

00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:09.717
are actually shaping
our ability to predict,

00:07:09.717 --> 00:07:11.220
and really they're shaping the engineering

00:07:11.220 --> 00:07:13.680
and they shape our ability
to control systems,

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:15.870
complex systems of interest.

00:07:15.870 --> 00:07:17.550
And today we're gonna talk
about two different systems,

00:07:17.550 --> 00:07:18.383
right?

00:07:18.383 --> 00:07:19.216
Genetics and evolution.

00:07:19.216 --> 00:07:20.190
We got this outstanding talk today

00:07:20.190 --> 00:07:21.990
that did a lot of the work for us.

00:07:21.990 --> 00:07:22.830
So I can skip a lot of that.

00:07:22.830 --> 00:07:25.290
So thank you for that, professor.

00:07:25.290 --> 00:07:26.123
And epidemics,

00:07:26.123 --> 00:07:29.340
these are both "biological" systems.

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:31.020
And they have many things in common.

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:31.853
So what we're gonna do

00:07:31.853 --> 00:07:33.580
is we're gonna kind of talk
about a little bit of a history

00:07:33.580 --> 00:07:35.670
of how we think about prediction

00:07:35.670 --> 00:07:37.110
and causation in these realms.

00:07:37.110 --> 00:07:38.940
Very, very little history.

00:07:38.940 --> 00:07:40.320
We're gonna talk about the modern times,

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:42.060
like what are the contemporary issues

00:07:42.060 --> 00:07:43.860
and challenges for thinking about these?

00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:45.270
And then I'm gonna hopefully end

00:07:45.270 --> 00:07:46.500
with a little bit of positivity

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:49.020
with regards to new
ways for thinking about

00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:51.090
how we can think about
prediction more carefully.

00:07:51.090 --> 00:07:51.923
And both of them,

00:07:51.923 --> 00:07:53.370
I'll introduce a new
theorem in the process,

00:07:53.370 --> 00:07:55.830
a funny theorem in the process.

00:07:55.830 --> 00:07:57.270
So history and genetics and evolution,

00:07:57.270 --> 00:07:58.620
we can kind of think about a lot of ways,

00:07:58.620 --> 00:07:59.460
a lot of really smart.

00:07:59.460 --> 00:08:00.690
David, I don't know how
you feel about this,

00:08:00.690 --> 00:08:03.450
but this is kind of one way of.

00:08:03.450 --> 00:08:07.290
One way of capturing
the era we've been in.

00:08:07.290 --> 00:08:09.900
I know it's, I think the
modern age probably needs it.

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:11.490
We're kind of like, in some ways,

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:12.600
we're in the age of genomics,

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:15.390
in some ways we're in a different era.

00:08:15.390 --> 00:08:16.340
But what we're gonna focus on now

00:08:16.340 --> 00:08:17.730
is the modern evolutionary synthesis.

00:08:17.730 --> 00:08:20.610
We got a really, really great
primer for that earlier.

00:08:20.610 --> 00:08:24.330
And I think importantly.

00:08:24.330 --> 00:08:27.060
This era is the one
that really transformed

00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:29.100
kind of biology into a quantitative field.

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:30.930
Of course, as you know,

00:08:30.930 --> 00:08:33.060
everybody who does empirical
science of any kind

00:08:33.060 --> 00:08:35.460
has been profoundly
influenced by Fisher's work.

00:08:36.450 --> 00:08:37.830
But really it was about the fusion

00:08:37.830 --> 00:08:40.260
of these Darwinian and Mendelian schools

00:08:40.260 --> 00:08:42.780
and kind of formulated evolution
as a quantitative science

00:08:42.780 --> 00:08:44.220
capable of describing and explaining

00:08:44.220 --> 00:08:45.270
the process of evolution.

00:08:45.270 --> 00:08:47.040
And so pretty much right away

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:49.140
when we got these quantitative tools

00:08:49.140 --> 00:08:52.440
to be able to potentially
treat a biological system

00:08:52.440 --> 00:08:55.540
like a physical one,
pretty much right away.

00:08:55.540 --> 00:08:59.880
People came up with these
new fundamental ways

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:01.980
to describe how populations work.

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:03.870
We were able to describe and understand

00:09:03.870 --> 00:09:06.150
what had happened for millennia

00:09:06.150 --> 00:09:07.680
with regards to artificial selection

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:09.440
and agriculture and domestication.

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:11.400
So it was really one of the most powerful

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:14.250
sets of understandings that
we've ever come up with,

00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:15.900
I think, and I will defend that.

00:09:15.900 --> 00:09:18.420
But of course, with that came
false prediction as well.

00:09:18.420 --> 00:09:20.220
And it came some errors as well.

00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:22.011
And of course, that we can cast

00:09:22.011 --> 00:09:24.510
that in the eugenic movement.

00:09:24.510 --> 00:09:26.580
The notion that we can take these kind of

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:28.274
discrete principles and apply

00:09:28.274 --> 00:09:30.153
them to human population.

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:33.990
So when we think about this,

00:09:33.990 --> 00:09:36.420
and I think this is the challenge.

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:38.550
When we're thinking about
how to understand systems

00:09:38.550 --> 00:09:40.170
well enough to understand cause and effect

00:09:40.170 --> 00:09:43.233
or causal inference or
understand prediction.

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:46.500
There's breakthroughs and
then there's questions

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:48.360
about what we do with those tools.

00:09:48.360 --> 00:09:51.120
And then there's kind
of technical misgivings

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:53.400
that oftentimes lead to certain problems.

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:56.130
And we see the signature
for both the beauty

00:09:56.130 --> 00:09:58.680
and the problems of this paradigm today.

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:01.740
So, I mean, this is a great
time to be in this profession.

00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:04.590
I think we're doing all
kinds of important things

00:10:04.590 --> 00:10:06.600
with genomic technology.

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:10.143
You might have saw the
approval of the first CRISPR,

00:10:11.250 --> 00:10:15.630
kind of truly CRISPR driven
therapy for sickle cell anemia

00:10:15.630 --> 00:10:16.920
that was just recently approved.

00:10:16.920 --> 00:10:19.050
So, I mean, it's really one
of the most amazing times

00:10:19.050 --> 00:10:22.230
to ever think about living
systems in this way.

00:10:22.230 --> 00:10:24.200
And so we can think about
things like genomic prediction

00:10:24.200 --> 00:10:26.160
and we think about genetic engineering

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:27.510
in ways we've never been able to.

00:10:27.510 --> 00:10:29.900
We can wire organisms mutation by mutation

00:10:29.900 --> 00:10:32.553
in order to kind of get
to phenotypes of interest.

00:10:33.630 --> 00:10:35.790
But of course, with that
comes things like this.

00:10:35.790 --> 00:10:38.070
And I don't know, we had
a conversation over dinner

00:10:38.070 --> 00:10:40.950
so I wanna make sure I put this in.

00:10:40.950 --> 00:10:43.440
Because this actually,
I don't know how many,

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:45.630
maybe raise hands, anybody seen this film?

00:10:45.630 --> 00:10:48.723
All right, I got a big raise back there.

00:10:49.980 --> 00:10:51.420
Well, good, I don't have to feel bad.

00:10:51.420 --> 00:10:53.010
This is pretty nerdy
what you're gonna see.

00:10:53.010 --> 00:10:54.910
So I actually wrote a paper about this

00:10:55.890 --> 00:10:57.490
that made the cover of genetics.

00:10:58.380 --> 00:11:00.300
And the idea here is
we kind of use the film

00:11:00.300 --> 00:11:02.940
to have a conversation about this exactly.

00:11:02.940 --> 00:11:06.030
The whole thing is how are
our predictive tools doing?

00:11:06.030 --> 00:11:08.820
We use the film to have a
contemporary conversation

00:11:08.820 --> 00:11:11.070
about where we are with
this sort of technology.

00:11:11.070 --> 00:11:14.250
And we actually review
things like embryo selection.

00:11:14.250 --> 00:11:15.560
What are the statistics about it?

00:11:15.560 --> 00:11:17.520
How good are we, how good are we not?

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:19.230
And they actually got a
lot of things quite right

00:11:19.230 --> 00:11:21.540
in the film 25 years ago.

00:11:21.540 --> 00:11:24.030
So again, right, cultural
relics and cultural things

00:11:24.030 --> 00:11:26.580
end up being this
important kind of substrate

00:11:26.580 --> 00:11:27.450
for us to be able to think

00:11:27.450 --> 00:11:29.640
about the contemporary technical issues

00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:31.480
when we're thinking about
causation and prediction.

00:11:31.480 --> 00:11:35.700
Okay, but of course, there's
many other conflicts.

00:11:35.700 --> 00:11:37.440
Right, many, many, many others.

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:39.420
So even things that I participated in

00:11:39.420 --> 00:11:41.010
like the precision medicine paradigm,

00:11:41.010 --> 00:11:43.290
the notion that we can treat
every single individual here

00:11:43.290 --> 00:11:45.570
according to their peculiar biology.

00:11:45.570 --> 00:11:46.403
I mean, it's just riddled

00:11:46.403 --> 00:11:48.330
with a lot of kind of
technical misgivings.

00:11:48.330 --> 00:11:51.000
We've already seen genetic
discrimination happen.

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:53.220
Obviously, some of you are a
part of these conversations

00:11:53.220 --> 00:11:55.380
about genetically modified technology.

00:11:55.380 --> 00:11:57.450
So these are some of the areas

00:11:57.450 --> 00:11:59.220
where despite all these
breakthroughs and excitement,

00:11:59.220 --> 00:12:02.850
we have a lot of contemporary
questions, right,

00:12:02.850 --> 00:12:05.160
about what it is that we're doing here.

00:12:05.160 --> 00:12:07.560
And of course, the rise
of extremism and fascism

00:12:07.560 --> 00:12:10.300
and race science, which of
course is another older.

00:12:10.300 --> 00:12:12.990
This goes back to eugenics,

00:12:12.990 --> 00:12:14.883
but of course, has been reanimated.

00:12:16.060 --> 00:12:19.380
So what we talked about here
was kind of this history

00:12:19.380 --> 00:12:21.960
of how genetics kind of has thought

00:12:21.960 --> 00:12:23.160
about this sort of problem

00:12:23.160 --> 00:12:24.600
and some contemporary things

00:12:24.600 --> 00:12:25.980
that we should be very, very excited about

00:12:25.980 --> 00:12:26.813
and some other things

00:12:26.813 --> 00:12:27.646
that we should be very,
very cautious about.

00:12:27.646 --> 00:12:29.880
And I think the same thing goes

00:12:29.880 --> 00:12:31.680
for how we think about the epidemics.

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:35.370
And of course, you know, long,
long history of epidemics.

00:12:35.370 --> 00:12:36.690
I'm gonna capture it in two slides,

00:12:36.690 --> 00:12:39.180
but kind of modern public
health as we understand it.

00:12:39.180 --> 00:12:41.640
Some of you know this story
about kind of the water pump

00:12:41.640 --> 00:12:43.900
and it was cholera and
it was the identification

00:12:43.900 --> 00:12:46.320
of the source being a water supply

00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:49.620
is the birth of by many
opinions of public health

00:12:49.620 --> 00:12:51.510
as we know it today.

00:12:51.510 --> 00:12:53.910
And of course, mathematical epidemiology,

00:12:53.910 --> 00:12:55.770
which is one of my paradigms,

00:12:55.770 --> 00:12:57.300
again, can be traced to many people.

00:12:57.300 --> 00:12:59.250
There's a long history going back,

00:12:59.250 --> 00:13:01.380
but I think the kind of one
of the modern iterations

00:13:01.380 --> 00:13:02.213
that some of us,

00:13:02.213 --> 00:13:04.170
I learned they actually
teach in physics here,

00:13:04.170 --> 00:13:06.630
which I was very, very happy
to hear in intro physics here.

00:13:06.630 --> 00:13:09.080
They actually teach dynamical systems

00:13:09.080 --> 00:13:11.370
to the biology majors.

00:13:11.370 --> 00:13:12.660
And it's basically this notion

00:13:12.660 --> 00:13:16.510
of how you can understand
and mathematize a population

00:13:17.790 --> 00:13:19.710
undergoing some kind
of infectious process.

00:13:19.710 --> 00:13:22.020
And this goes back to 1927, okay.

00:13:22.020 --> 00:13:23.610
So again, long history doing that,

00:13:23.610 --> 00:13:25.290
a lot of successes, lots of failures,

00:13:25.290 --> 00:13:27.000
but we can kind of animate that now

00:13:27.000 --> 00:13:29.250
and what those contentions
and challenges are

00:13:29.250 --> 00:13:30.360
with regards to,

00:13:30.360 --> 00:13:31.650
'cause the point is when
you model something,

00:13:31.650 --> 00:13:34.020
presumably you understand
what its character is

00:13:34.020 --> 00:13:35.640
such that you can potentially predict it.

00:13:35.640 --> 00:13:36.690
Like that's the point.

00:13:36.690 --> 00:13:37.620
You wanna be able to understand

00:13:37.620 --> 00:13:39.510
who you need to vaccinate and where,

00:13:39.510 --> 00:13:41.130
where you need to intervene
and what have you.

00:13:41.130 --> 00:13:42.743
And I think this of course
has been animated here

00:13:42.743 --> 00:13:44.843
in case you haven't been paying attention.

00:13:46.440 --> 00:13:48.480
And of course, the roles of modeling

00:13:48.480 --> 00:13:49.980
and what we were predicting and how

00:13:49.980 --> 00:13:53.760
was really one of the central
conflicts of the pandemic era

00:13:53.760 --> 00:13:54.780
and remains so.

00:13:54.780 --> 00:13:57.660
And I think mathematical
mind was kind of on trial,

00:13:57.660 --> 00:13:59.640
if you will, in this sort of way.

00:13:59.640 --> 00:14:01.230
And so it had to kind of,

00:14:01.230 --> 00:14:04.950
we had to kind of reflect
on what are we modeling?

00:14:04.950 --> 00:14:06.180
How are we modeling it?

00:14:06.180 --> 00:14:07.290
Are we using the right tools?

00:14:07.290 --> 00:14:09.600
What are the assumptions
that we're making.

00:14:09.600 --> 00:14:11.340
This ended up being something
very, very important

00:14:11.340 --> 00:14:12.270
that was essential.

00:14:12.270 --> 00:14:13.470
Now, in the face of this,

00:14:13.470 --> 00:14:15.390
I did some mathematical modeling on this,

00:14:15.390 --> 00:14:16.740
but most of my work, again,

00:14:16.740 --> 00:14:18.840
we're gonna return why I
think this sort of meeting

00:14:18.840 --> 00:14:22.080
and why I'm so grateful
for this sort of gathering

00:14:22.080 --> 00:14:24.000
is the multidisciplinarity
is not just nominal.

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:26.340
You really actually learn from it.

00:14:26.340 --> 00:14:28.820
You learn what the important
questions are from engaging

00:14:28.820 --> 00:14:30.240
because I did a little bit of this,

00:14:30.240 --> 00:14:33.900
but I did much more of this,
much more public engagement.

00:14:33.900 --> 00:14:36.090
I became kind of a misinformation warrior

00:14:36.090 --> 00:14:37.080
during the pandemic.

00:14:37.080 --> 00:14:37.980
So I did some mathematical,

00:14:37.980 --> 00:14:41.203
and what I learned about the pandemic

00:14:43.620 --> 00:14:48.180
and the way we engage it is that

00:14:48.180 --> 00:14:49.110
there are several challenges

00:14:49.110 --> 00:14:52.410
that I think that are
common to both genetics

00:14:52.410 --> 00:14:53.670
and epidemiology.

00:14:53.670 --> 00:14:56.460
So when you think about
causation and prediction,

00:14:56.460 --> 00:14:58.980
I think the ethical is the
technical in some ways.

00:14:58.980 --> 00:15:00.870
So I think these are separate paradigms

00:15:00.870 --> 00:15:02.580
with separate grammars.

00:15:02.580 --> 00:15:04.260
But part of the conversation,

00:15:04.260 --> 00:15:06.210
a lot of the things that
we think are ethical

00:15:06.210 --> 00:15:09.840
are actually rooted in technical
challenges that we have,

00:15:09.840 --> 00:15:11.787
in being able to properly predict

00:15:11.787 --> 00:15:13.560
the shape of an epidemic

00:15:13.560 --> 00:15:14.940
or being able to properly predict

00:15:14.940 --> 00:15:17.340
what the phenotypic
effects are of a mutation

00:15:17.340 --> 00:15:18.360
or what's in the genome

00:15:18.360 --> 00:15:21.663
or the relationship between
genotype and phenotype.

00:15:22.860 --> 00:15:25.400
And, you know, it's rooted in,

00:15:25.400 --> 00:15:26.490
we might be really swell people,

00:15:26.490 --> 00:15:28.170
but our ideas might be bad.

00:15:28.170 --> 00:15:31.620
I mean, in particular
in the genomics context.

00:15:31.620 --> 00:15:33.810
You might have good intentions,

00:15:33.810 --> 00:15:35.430
and I'm not here to judge
anybody's intentions

00:15:35.430 --> 00:15:36.930
with regards to being able to predict

00:15:36.930 --> 00:15:40.140
whether or not baby A or B
will get into Yale or not.

00:15:40.140 --> 00:15:41.853
You might have good intentions.

00:15:43.530 --> 00:15:45.750
But the ideas that you're using

00:15:45.750 --> 00:15:47.520
to be able to kind of, you know,

00:15:47.520 --> 00:15:50.310
influence that prediction,
of course, might be bad.

00:15:50.310 --> 00:15:51.930
And of course, this applies very firmly

00:15:51.930 --> 00:15:55.830
to a lot of the conversation
with regards to ethical AI,

00:15:55.830 --> 00:15:57.420
right, but I was talking
to someone, I think you,

00:15:57.420 --> 00:15:59.163
about timescale yesterday.

00:16:00.200 --> 00:16:02.550
The timescale consequences
and effect on accountability,

00:16:02.550 --> 00:16:04.770
sometimes the negative
consequences are too downstream

00:16:04.770 --> 00:16:06.960
for the people who are, right.

00:16:06.960 --> 00:16:10.620
So if I mispredict the
shape of an epidemic

00:16:10.620 --> 00:16:12.270
like two years from now,

00:16:12.270 --> 00:16:15.720
and I come up with a harmful policy

00:16:15.720 --> 00:16:19.710
is that timescale for which
that's wrong and causes harm,

00:16:19.710 --> 00:16:21.630
does that match up with
me being held accountable

00:16:21.630 --> 00:16:23.730
for my bad decision-making up front.

00:16:23.730 --> 00:16:24.780
So this is one of the problems.

00:16:24.780 --> 00:16:28.590
There's not a lot of cost
for me getting things wrong

00:16:28.590 --> 00:16:30.240
when I'm making these
incorrect predictions,

00:16:30.240 --> 00:16:32.670
and that's sure as heck
goes in the genomic context

00:16:32.670 --> 00:16:34.530
when you don't see the consequences

00:16:34.530 --> 00:16:36.150
for your actions for generations.

00:16:36.150 --> 00:16:37.400
So I think there's that challenge

00:16:37.400 --> 00:16:38.763
that we have to deal with.

00:16:39.600 --> 00:16:40.740
But more than that, are we thinking

00:16:40.740 --> 00:16:43.080
about complex biological
systems correctly.

00:16:43.080 --> 00:16:45.810
And this is where we'll go next.

00:16:45.810 --> 00:16:47.850
I have some kind of frameworks
for how to think about this.

00:16:47.850 --> 00:16:49.260
And so you're understanding the role

00:16:49.260 --> 00:16:50.380
of constraining context and shaping,

00:16:50.380 --> 00:16:52.320
it can really help our predictions.

00:16:52.320 --> 00:16:53.880
I think there are frameworks
and understandings

00:16:53.880 --> 00:16:56.550
and concepts in both
genetics and epidemics

00:16:56.550 --> 00:16:59.790
that can actually help us
kind of wade through this.

00:16:59.790 --> 00:17:01.260
One of the things I try to fight through

00:17:01.260 --> 00:17:02.280
when I think about these sorts

00:17:02.280 --> 00:17:03.780
of complex biological systems

00:17:03.780 --> 00:17:05.730
with a lot of different
process of information

00:17:05.730 --> 00:17:07.350
is to not be nihilistic.

00:17:07.350 --> 00:17:08.550
The goal, the point is not

00:17:08.550 --> 00:17:09.990
that everything is hopelessly complicated

00:17:09.990 --> 00:17:11.490
and so we shouldn't bother.

00:17:11.490 --> 00:17:12.570
Maybe some of you think that.

00:17:12.570 --> 00:17:14.910
But like that's not where I live.

00:17:14.910 --> 00:17:16.740
I think it's more about understanding

00:17:16.740 --> 00:17:18.570
what the specific roles of those contexts.

00:17:18.570 --> 00:17:20.010
So we'll talk about these two things.

00:17:20.010 --> 00:17:20.843
What are some constraints

00:17:20.843 --> 00:17:22.890
that govern complex biological systems?

00:17:22.890 --> 00:17:25.350
In genetics, we have gene
by environment interactions.

00:17:25.350 --> 00:17:26.250
You might have heard that one.

00:17:26.250 --> 00:17:28.440
That one is reasonably popular.

00:17:28.440 --> 00:17:30.720
And that is the notion
that you can't really tell

00:17:30.720 --> 00:17:33.480
what the consequences of a
gene or a mutation are doing.

00:17:33.480 --> 00:17:34.313
You need to know something

00:17:34.313 --> 00:17:36.420
about the environment you're in.

00:17:36.420 --> 00:17:38.340
I think the second one is
what we'll talk briefly

00:17:38.340 --> 00:17:40.350
about today, gene by gene interactions.

00:17:40.350 --> 00:17:43.230
It's a concept called epistasis
and it's a little bit odd.

00:17:43.230 --> 00:17:44.763
So we'll spend a second there.

00:17:45.720 --> 00:17:47.640
With epidemics,
host-parasite interactions.

00:17:47.640 --> 00:17:50.670
I think anybody that's ever
been sick knows what that means.

00:17:50.670 --> 00:17:52.230
But host-host interactions is the way

00:17:52.230 --> 00:17:53.220
that people are interacting,

00:17:53.220 --> 00:17:55.320
influence the dynamics
of disease, you bet.

00:17:55.320 --> 00:17:56.970
But then there's other social forces

00:17:56.970 --> 00:17:59.040
that can interact with how hosts

00:17:59.040 --> 00:18:00.090
or interact with other hosts,

00:18:00.090 --> 00:18:02.130
and by extension, are
interacting with parasites.

00:18:02.130 --> 00:18:04.620
And these are the sorts of
interactions, like I said,

00:18:04.620 --> 00:18:07.860
that are actually shaping these
complex biological systems.

00:18:07.860 --> 00:18:11.790
And by extension, influence causal change,

00:18:11.790 --> 00:18:15.000
and influence predictive, the
possibility of prediction.

00:18:15.000 --> 00:18:17.493
Okay, so we'll start with genetics.

00:18:18.360 --> 00:18:21.840
Now we'll start with this
concept called epistasis.

00:18:21.840 --> 00:18:23.600
It's been called the
dark matter of biology

00:18:23.600 --> 00:18:25.800
for the physicists.

00:18:25.800 --> 00:18:26.910
Or the dark matter of genetics.

00:18:26.910 --> 00:18:28.170
Now what is it exactly?

00:18:28.170 --> 00:18:29.820
This is a great colloquial definition

00:18:29.820 --> 00:18:31.620
from a mentor and friend, Dan Weinreich,

00:18:31.620 --> 00:18:34.237
a theoretical population geneticist.

00:18:34.237 --> 00:18:37.710
"The surprise at the phenotype
when mutations are combined

00:18:37.710 --> 00:18:41.130
given the constituent
mutation's individual effects."

00:18:41.130 --> 00:18:43.800
I know what mutation A does by itself.

00:18:43.800 --> 00:18:45.210
I know what mutation B does by itself.

00:18:45.210 --> 00:18:47.220
I put 'em together and I
get a whoa, a surprise.

00:18:47.220 --> 00:18:49.440
And that's like legitimately what it is.

00:18:49.440 --> 00:18:52.140
I have this fun graduate student analogy.

00:18:52.140 --> 00:18:54.360
I have two extraordinarily
talented graduate students

00:18:54.360 --> 00:18:56.370
in my lab when they combine.

00:18:56.370 --> 00:18:58.953
So these are their, is there a pointer?

00:19:01.080 --> 00:19:01.913
It's on here.

00:19:04.680 --> 00:19:06.390
Oh, yes.

00:19:06.390 --> 00:19:08.910
Right, so both of them have, are positive.

00:19:08.910 --> 00:19:11.160
This is kind of a, the y-axis
is just how good they are.

00:19:11.160 --> 00:19:13.140
So they're extraordinary, both of them.

00:19:13.140 --> 00:19:16.560
You put them together, you put
them together experimentally,

00:19:16.560 --> 00:19:17.943
right, and you get this,

00:19:19.350 --> 00:19:21.240
you get this additive effect.

00:19:21.240 --> 00:19:23.010
As in you just add 'em together.

00:19:23.010 --> 00:19:26.100
When they're coding together,
you get this extraordinary

00:19:26.100 --> 00:19:27.930
kind of non-linear positive interaction,

00:19:27.930 --> 00:19:29.640
which means they code
together beautifully.

00:19:29.640 --> 00:19:31.230
But when I ask them to
write a paper together,

00:19:31.230 --> 00:19:33.390
they actually like have
a negative interaction.

00:19:33.390 --> 00:19:34.920
And they get less work done together

00:19:34.920 --> 00:19:35.753
than either of them would have

00:19:35.753 --> 00:19:37.023
been done individually.

00:19:38.040 --> 00:19:39.840
And that's really what we're
talking about with mutations.

00:19:39.840 --> 00:19:41.620
I mean, I think they have
these sorts of different

00:19:41.620 --> 00:19:44.640
interaction effects between mutations.

00:19:44.640 --> 00:19:45.990
Now, why is this important?

00:19:45.990 --> 00:19:48.330
I mean, it's just completely
rewires how we think

00:19:48.330 --> 00:19:50.640
about the importance of the
individual mutation or gene

00:19:50.640 --> 00:19:52.650
in dictating phenotype.

00:19:52.650 --> 00:19:54.690
If all your genotes,
if all your phenotypes

00:19:54.690 --> 00:19:57.060
depend on interactions between genes,

00:19:57.060 --> 00:19:58.670
and we're talking about 20,000 and 50,000

00:19:58.670 --> 00:20:01.020
and 100,000 genes, then the problem,

00:20:01.020 --> 00:20:03.540
I mean, astronomical is too small

00:20:03.540 --> 00:20:05.190
to think about how quickly this

00:20:05.190 --> 00:20:07.980
combinatorial explosion
creates a mess here.

00:20:07.980 --> 00:20:10.840
Well, unless we have some
tools for this crowd.

00:20:10.840 --> 00:20:13.200
Yeah, we have, the point of this is just,

00:20:13.200 --> 00:20:15.840
we have a whole host of statistical tools

00:20:15.840 --> 00:20:18.180
to be able to resolve out
higher-order interactions.

00:20:18.180 --> 00:20:19.470
I think, I look around the room,

00:20:19.470 --> 00:20:20.640
and look, you all have them.

00:20:20.640 --> 00:20:21.570
In fact, we've borrowed this

00:20:21.570 --> 00:20:22.950
from theoretical computer science.

00:20:22.950 --> 00:20:24.630
This is the Walsh-Hadamard transform,

00:20:24.630 --> 00:20:27.930
which comes from noise stuff

00:20:27.930 --> 00:20:29.370
in engineering and computer science.

00:20:29.370 --> 00:20:31.710
But we now apply it to
how we can resolve out

00:20:31.710 --> 00:20:33.510
and come up with the basic algebra

00:20:33.510 --> 00:20:36.330
for how we can resolve out
how epistatic interactions

00:20:36.330 --> 00:20:37.893
are occurring between genes.

00:20:40.620 --> 00:20:43.170
But again, this nihilism question.

00:20:43.170 --> 00:20:44.970
Is this a nihilistic understanding?

00:20:44.970 --> 00:20:46.740
If everything has to interact
with everything else,

00:20:46.740 --> 00:20:48.960
isn't that kind of a hopeless feeling.

00:20:48.960 --> 00:20:50.700
But thankfully, there's some new concepts

00:20:50.700 --> 00:20:52.320
with regards to epistasis

00:20:52.320 --> 00:20:53.610
that I think give us rhyme and reason,

00:20:53.610 --> 00:20:55.590
and that's called global epistasis.

00:20:55.590 --> 00:20:58.170
This basically says that
there are fundamental rules

00:20:58.170 --> 00:21:00.900
governing how those interactions

00:21:00.900 --> 00:21:02.490
between those graduate students happen,

00:21:02.490 --> 00:21:04.950
such that I can potentially predict them.

00:21:04.950 --> 00:21:06.420
So even though they're nonlinear,

00:21:06.420 --> 00:21:08.670
and they feel surprising when it happens,

00:21:08.670 --> 00:21:11.010
there's actually an underlying physics

00:21:11.010 --> 00:21:12.660
of kind of interacting information,

00:21:12.660 --> 00:21:14.820
and this is pioneered by Alvaro Sanchez,

00:21:14.820 --> 00:21:18.570
my close colleague, and
we've done this work.

00:21:18.570 --> 00:21:21.180
We've done a bunch of
work on this recently.

00:21:21.180 --> 00:21:25.050
So they also often
follow systemic patterns

00:21:25.050 --> 00:21:26.463
that enable predictability,

00:21:27.800 --> 00:21:29.910
and this kind of changes our relationship

00:21:29.910 --> 00:21:32.670
with a concept that would
otherwise be nihilistic.

00:21:32.670 --> 00:21:33.810
We now have this understanding

00:21:33.810 --> 00:21:36.690
that, wow, we can actually
do something about this

00:21:36.690 --> 00:21:37.720
to understand this phenomenon,

00:21:37.720 --> 00:21:39.630
and now this is being animated

00:21:39.630 --> 00:21:41.780
all across kind of genetics and genomics

00:21:41.780 --> 00:21:44.490
to add rhyme and reason to phenomenon

00:21:44.490 --> 00:21:46.983
that seem to undermine predictability.

00:21:51.770 --> 00:21:53.040
So, again, global epistasis suggests

00:21:53.040 --> 00:21:55.170
that there's fundamental rules

00:21:55.170 --> 00:21:56.490
for these sorts of interactions,

00:21:56.490 --> 00:21:58.500
such that they can be predicted,

00:21:58.500 --> 00:21:59.970
and we'll kind of know not to make them,

00:21:59.970 --> 00:22:01.570
write papers together, but they.

00:22:02.970 --> 00:22:05.910
Now, shifting to the epidemic context.

00:22:05.910 --> 00:22:08.430
We talked about host-parasite
interactions, HOSO.

00:22:08.430 --> 00:22:10.020
We're gonna talk about
the sorts of constraints

00:22:10.020 --> 00:22:12.330
that influence them, and
what are some different ways

00:22:12.330 --> 00:22:13.500
we can think about prediction

00:22:13.500 --> 00:22:15.870
and causation in this system, all right?

00:22:15.870 --> 00:22:17.190
So one of the things that I've studied

00:22:17.190 --> 00:22:19.200
is the kind of mathematical
and computational models

00:22:19.200 --> 00:22:20.820
of hepatitis C virus.

00:22:20.820 --> 00:22:23.610
We call this the silent
epidemic for a bunch of reasons,

00:22:23.610 --> 00:22:25.650
right, having to do with
the people it affects,

00:22:25.650 --> 00:22:27.600
it's people who inject drugs,

00:22:27.600 --> 00:22:30.000
and how, like, the natural
history of the disease

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:34.320
kind of can make it
challenging to understand,

00:22:34.320 --> 00:22:36.930
but nonetheless, it's extremely important.

00:22:36.930 --> 00:22:38.290
And so what we did is we actually took,

00:22:38.290 --> 00:22:40.800
we created a mathematical
model that if you look at,

00:22:40.800 --> 00:22:43.020
if you think about a disease
that's spread by people

00:22:43.020 --> 00:22:45.330
who are sharing drug paraphernalia,

00:22:45.330 --> 00:22:46.920
it has features in common with

00:22:46.920 --> 00:22:49.020
a vector-borne disease.

00:22:49.020 --> 00:22:51.290
This is exactly what
we did mathematically.

00:22:51.290 --> 00:22:56.290
We basically, right,
published here in 2019,

00:22:56.790 --> 00:22:59.970
but something very important
happened in this work.

00:22:59.970 --> 00:23:01.230
When we were doing this work,

00:23:01.230 --> 00:23:06.230
I found myself in Reddit forums
of people who were users,

00:23:06.900 --> 00:23:09.300
right, because I'm trying
to understand some things

00:23:09.300 --> 00:23:11.910
about the way that the
disease is transmitted,

00:23:11.910 --> 00:23:14.490
and in that, I kind of,
I got very, very guilty

00:23:14.490 --> 00:23:16.117
'cause I said, "Here,
I'm trying to mathematize

00:23:16.117 --> 00:23:18.337
"something with all of this social import.

00:23:18.337 --> 00:23:20.940
"This is something that's
really, really affecting people."

00:23:20.940 --> 00:23:22.950
Okay, now, right, part of the reason

00:23:22.950 --> 00:23:24.450
why this became important mathematically

00:23:24.450 --> 00:23:26.400
is I began to think and learn more

00:23:26.400 --> 00:23:30.270
about the way social forces
are actually crafting the thing

00:23:30.270 --> 00:23:32.850
that I'm trying to mathematize.

00:23:32.850 --> 00:23:37.470
So that ends up being a
serious and important dimension

00:23:37.470 --> 00:23:39.840
for how we're thinking about modeling

00:23:39.840 --> 00:23:42.090
and predicting the shape
of these epidemics.

00:23:42.090 --> 00:23:44.750
I can't possibly understand
anything about disease

00:23:44.750 --> 00:23:46.740
if I'm not engaged with the forces

00:23:46.740 --> 00:23:48.690
that are actually influencing the host.

00:23:49.860 --> 00:23:53.640
So again, right, a reason why to engage

00:23:53.640 --> 00:23:54.900
these different disciplines.

00:23:54.900 --> 00:23:56.760
And of course, there's
other social forces,

00:23:56.760 --> 00:23:57.900
and I began to work on this.

00:23:57.900 --> 00:23:59.170
I've worked on this, right,

00:23:59.170 --> 00:24:04.170
how kind of policy
regarding prison influences

00:24:04.230 --> 00:24:08.073
the dynamics of a pandemic
in multiple dimensions.

00:24:09.430 --> 00:24:12.240
So what we're seeing here is, right,

00:24:12.240 --> 00:24:15.900
in the context of epidemiology was, right,

00:24:15.900 --> 00:24:17.730
I'm interested, I have a complex system

00:24:17.730 --> 00:24:19.490
composed of all of these elements,

00:24:19.490 --> 00:24:24.490
and I wanna know how to predict it.

00:24:25.110 --> 00:24:27.720
We can think about
causation in this system,

00:24:27.720 --> 00:24:30.030
and we're identifying
all of these constraints

00:24:30.030 --> 00:24:33.000
that influence what those
predictions will be.

00:24:33.000 --> 00:24:35.040
And this paper here is
really one of my favorite,

00:24:35.040 --> 00:24:38.100
and it's admittedly by
two good friends of mine,

00:24:38.100 --> 00:24:39.780
but I think it's one of
the most important papers

00:24:39.780 --> 00:24:43.680
I think I've read in
epidemiology in quite some time.

00:24:43.680 --> 00:24:45.900
And they introduced
this notion from physics

00:24:45.900 --> 00:24:48.210
called permutation entropy, right,

00:24:48.210 --> 00:24:49.470
which kind of has this other life,

00:24:49.470 --> 00:24:51.630
but they apply it to
the case of epidemics.

00:24:51.630 --> 00:24:52.500
And this is what they say.

00:24:52.500 --> 00:24:55.050
Here's the model that we
did for hepatitis C virus.

00:24:55.050 --> 00:24:56.470
And so we don't, you know, for those,

00:24:56.470 --> 00:24:58.200
one of the innovations that we did

00:24:58.200 --> 00:25:01.110
is we modeled needles
as their own population,

00:25:01.110 --> 00:25:03.060
and so we can actually
think about what happens

00:25:03.060 --> 00:25:04.920
when you take needles out of a population,

00:25:04.920 --> 00:25:08.010
for example, with a safe injection sites,

00:25:08.010 --> 00:25:09.300
or with needle exchange,

00:25:09.300 --> 00:25:10.870
these sorts of public health interventions

00:25:10.870 --> 00:25:12.870
that allow you to actually directly deal

00:25:12.870 --> 00:25:14.250
with the problem of needles.

00:25:14.250 --> 00:25:16.230
That was one of the kind
of conceptual innovations

00:25:16.230 --> 00:25:18.030
to this mathematical model.

00:25:18.030 --> 00:25:19.800
What this paper says is that

00:25:19.800 --> 00:25:23.823
if you wanna predict the
shaping of an epidemic, okay,

00:25:25.170 --> 00:25:30.170
the problem is the
parameters themself change

00:25:30.510 --> 00:25:33.540
as the epidemic is happening.

00:25:33.540 --> 00:25:37.110
And so you are hopeless
in trying to predict

00:25:37.110 --> 00:25:39.990
an epidemic past a few days.

00:25:39.990 --> 00:25:41.160
And of course, it'll depend on disease,

00:25:41.160 --> 00:25:42.360
it'll depend on.

00:25:42.360 --> 00:25:43.890
But it introduced kind of this idea

00:25:43.890 --> 00:25:46.770
that it really is a fruitless venture

00:25:46.770 --> 00:25:48.720
to try to come up with a universal,

00:25:48.720 --> 00:25:50.880
long-term predictive algorithm.

00:25:50.880 --> 00:25:51.720
It doesn't work that way

00:25:51.720 --> 00:25:53.700
because the parameters are
changing too, too fast.

00:25:53.700 --> 00:25:55.770
And I think this is the innovation here.

00:25:55.770 --> 00:25:57.480
And I think it's had a big effect on me

00:25:57.480 --> 00:25:59.310
because this is the sort of thing.

00:25:59.310 --> 00:26:01.470
It's not that we can't predict,

00:26:01.470 --> 00:26:03.690
it's that perhaps we should be changing

00:26:03.690 --> 00:26:06.570
what our goals are for prediction.

00:26:06.570 --> 00:26:08.640
It's not that we can't
understand causal chains,

00:26:08.640 --> 00:26:10.320
it's how far apart they are

00:26:10.320 --> 00:26:11.790
when we're talking about the actors

00:26:11.790 --> 00:26:15.153
and their consequences in
epidemiological systems.

00:26:17.070 --> 00:26:19.770
And so this is what we
mean about prediction.

00:26:19.770 --> 00:26:23.280
Now, we talked about this
in various frameworks.

00:26:23.280 --> 00:26:25.440
We talked about this in genetic systems,

00:26:25.440 --> 00:26:26.273
the sorts of things

00:26:26.273 --> 00:26:28.470
that both undermine
prediction in genetics,

00:26:28.470 --> 00:26:31.050
but potentially, right,
there's utility here.

00:26:31.050 --> 00:26:32.580
The global epistasis framework

00:26:32.580 --> 00:26:34.410
potentially allows us to predict.

00:26:34.410 --> 00:26:36.590
In the context of epidemics,

00:26:36.590 --> 00:26:38.430
we can talk about something similar.

00:26:38.430 --> 00:26:42.300
I think the permutation
entropy framework helps us.

00:26:42.300 --> 00:26:45.180
And what I'll leave is with
something very, very, right,

00:26:45.180 --> 00:26:46.170
with a concept.

00:26:46.170 --> 00:26:48.690
This is my son and he's a toddler.

00:26:48.690 --> 00:26:51.210
Anybody that's ever been
around a toddler knows

00:26:51.210 --> 00:26:52.860
predicting what your child is gonna be

00:26:52.860 --> 00:26:54.120
is a very, very important,

00:26:54.120 --> 00:26:56.790
like that's kind of part
of what being a parent is.

00:26:56.790 --> 00:26:58.560
I have a prediction that he's
gonna live with me at 30,

00:26:58.560 --> 00:27:01.590
but you know, by the way he
acts now, but you never know.

00:27:01.590 --> 00:27:03.893
Now, as useful as it would
be for me to be able to,

00:27:03.893 --> 00:27:05.490
this is very important,

00:27:05.490 --> 00:27:07.590
as useful as it would be
able to be for me to predict

00:27:07.590 --> 00:27:08.760
that he's gonna be quarterback

00:27:08.760 --> 00:27:11.070
for the University of Michigan,

00:27:11.070 --> 00:27:12.750
it's actually much more useful

00:27:12.750 --> 00:27:14.100
for me to be able to predict

00:27:14.100 --> 00:27:15.873
when he'll be able to nap tomorrow.

00:27:18.180 --> 00:27:20.990
So the notion that a
long-term prediction is better

00:27:20.990 --> 00:27:24.240
or holistic is better is just not true.

00:27:24.240 --> 00:27:25.890
I think we should learn to be, right,

00:27:25.890 --> 00:27:29.520
somewhat satisfied or in fact,

00:27:29.520 --> 00:27:32.430
goal shift to tuning our expectations

00:27:32.430 --> 00:27:34.570
in particularly with regards to time scale

00:27:34.570 --> 00:27:36.420
for our predictions.

00:27:36.420 --> 00:27:39.810
And that is, right, if we have
very, very, very, very strong

00:27:39.810 --> 00:27:43.200
predictive ability for tomorrow,

00:27:43.200 --> 00:27:44.640
that's actually actionable,

00:27:44.640 --> 00:27:47.340
that's not actually any
less rigorous technically,

00:27:47.340 --> 00:27:50.430
and I think it completely
changes how we think

00:27:50.430 --> 00:27:53.043
about a lot of these
complex biological systems.

00:27:54.780 --> 00:27:55.683
Yeah, anyway.

00:27:57.120 --> 00:27:58.110
Great.

00:27:58.110 --> 00:28:01.277
(audience applauding)

00:28:03.970 --> 00:28:06.300
- Very interesting to point out

00:28:06.300 --> 00:28:09.210
that that time scale over
which predictions are made

00:28:09.210 --> 00:28:11.530
are sort of fundamentally relevant

00:28:13.070 --> 00:28:17.430
because in public policy
frameworks and contexts,

00:28:17.430 --> 00:28:19.470
you know, you need to make decisions

00:28:19.470 --> 00:28:22.830
and it's fine to say for
astronomical systems,

00:28:22.830 --> 00:28:25.470
I can predict two galaxies that will merge

00:28:25.470 --> 00:28:27.540
in 10 to the seven years or whatever.

00:28:27.540 --> 00:28:30.840
So yeah, thanks for
bringing that into focus.

00:28:30.840 --> 00:28:31.743
Questions?

00:28:37.320 --> 00:28:38.220
- Great.

00:28:38.220 --> 00:28:40.320
Thanks so much for your very rich talk.

00:28:40.320 --> 00:28:42.540
Yeah, I just wanted to maybe
think together a bit more

00:28:42.540 --> 00:28:45.690
just about constraints
and the role they play

00:28:45.690 --> 00:28:47.400
'cause it seems to me there's kind of,

00:28:47.400 --> 00:28:48.790
so some people just think of constraints

00:28:48.790 --> 00:28:51.423
as like additional causes or stable causes

00:28:51.423 --> 00:28:53.190
that you haven't measured.

00:28:53.190 --> 00:28:54.720
I guess my suspicion has always been

00:28:54.720 --> 00:28:55.890
that they're much richer.

00:28:55.890 --> 00:28:57.510
That's not just something
like causal variable

00:28:57.510 --> 00:29:00.390
that you could have intervened
on that you didn't include,

00:29:00.390 --> 00:29:01.920
but can be something, I mean,

00:29:01.920 --> 00:29:04.110
we have historical
constraints or long patterns

00:29:04.110 --> 00:29:07.590
or sometimes these timescale
dependent behaviors,

00:29:07.590 --> 00:29:11.520
it really feels more like a
fundamental characterization

00:29:11.520 --> 00:29:13.380
of the system and there's
no translatability.

00:29:13.380 --> 00:29:15.000
So I was just actually trying,

00:29:15.000 --> 00:29:17.250
I'm really trying just to open
up a conversation about it.

00:29:17.250 --> 00:29:19.460
If you have a thought about
how to model constraints,

00:29:19.460 --> 00:29:20.670
is it just, yeah.

00:29:20.670 --> 00:29:21.660
- Yeah, no, no, thank you for that.

00:29:21.660 --> 00:29:22.910
I mean, I think, you know,

00:29:22.910 --> 00:29:26.460
take a problem like antibiotic
resistance evolution,

00:29:26.460 --> 00:29:27.540
which is something that I think about.

00:29:27.540 --> 00:29:28.440
It's a complex problem.

00:29:28.440 --> 00:29:29.280
It's an evolutionary problem.

00:29:29.280 --> 00:29:30.120
It's a molecular problem.

00:29:30.120 --> 00:29:30.953
It's a protein problem.

00:29:30.953 --> 00:29:33.720
We've studied that problem
from the Angstrom scale.

00:29:33.720 --> 00:29:35.160
When you think about
kind of the likelihood

00:29:35.160 --> 00:29:36.270
of evolving something like that,

00:29:36.270 --> 00:29:38.370
this is where
multidisciplinarity is essential.

00:29:38.370 --> 00:29:40.890
It's not just like fun to talk to people.

00:29:40.890 --> 00:29:43.390
Half the problem is people's
willingness to take drugs

00:29:43.390 --> 00:29:46.023
on a certain schedule.

00:29:46.980 --> 00:29:47.870
So if you are not,

00:29:47.870 --> 00:29:51.540
that is a constraint
that the system, right,

00:29:51.540 --> 00:29:53.790
that is actually dictating the pace

00:29:53.790 --> 00:29:56.940
and the direction of evolution
of antimicrobial resistance.

00:29:56.940 --> 00:29:59.130
So I think I like your
definition in the sense of,

00:29:59.130 --> 00:30:01.260
it's all, I mean, it's all of the things

00:30:01.260 --> 00:30:03.887
that we don't need to know all of them.

00:30:03.887 --> 00:30:06.900
But we need to know the ones
that meaningfully influence

00:30:06.900 --> 00:30:09.030
the dynamics that we are trying to predict

00:30:09.030 --> 00:30:11.850
and/or establish causal chains between.

00:30:11.850 --> 00:30:13.230
So that's how I think about constraints.

00:30:13.230 --> 00:30:16.410
And I think the challenge is the silos.

00:30:16.410 --> 00:30:19.050
Either we're not trained
to think very, very broadly

00:30:19.050 --> 00:30:20.370
about some of these other dimensions

00:30:20.370 --> 00:30:21.720
or we're unwilling to do so,

00:30:21.720 --> 00:30:23.820
or we don't have people
who bring them together

00:30:23.820 --> 00:30:24.750
in this sort of way.

00:30:24.750 --> 00:30:27.633
And I think this is why a lot of my work,

00:30:28.990 --> 00:30:31.140
I've gained tremendously from being able

00:30:31.140 --> 00:30:33.483
to open up that dimensionality.

00:30:35.040 --> 00:30:36.690
- Thank you.

00:30:36.690 --> 00:30:37.523
Shyam.

00:30:40.710 --> 00:30:43.590
- The problem of prediction

00:30:43.590 --> 00:30:48.360
in sociobiological systems like epidemics.

00:30:48.360 --> 00:30:52.653
Where there's a feedback loop,

00:30:54.400 --> 00:30:59.400
prediction itself can cause conditions

00:31:00.220 --> 00:31:02.853
that will make that prediction untrue,

00:31:03.780 --> 00:31:07.773
which is very different from
pure physical systems on.

00:31:10.560 --> 00:31:11.393
- More than anything.

00:31:11.393 --> 00:31:13.503
I mean, I think that's true, we see that.

00:31:15.360 --> 00:31:17.250
- What do we do on,

00:31:17.250 --> 00:31:19.040
how do we deal with the
problem of prediction

00:31:19.040 --> 00:31:20.943
in social systems?

00:31:22.200 --> 00:31:23.450
- No, I mean, again, I'm not a,

00:31:23.450 --> 00:31:25.710
believe it, I'm the not negative one

00:31:25.710 --> 00:31:27.900
about these sorts of
things in a lot of rooms.

00:31:27.900 --> 00:31:28.740
Yeah, I mean,

00:31:28.740 --> 00:31:30.517
mathematical modeling has
got a lot of things right.

00:31:30.517 --> 00:31:32.100
There are a lot of conditions

00:31:32.100 --> 00:31:33.900
that it actually was able
to properly characterize

00:31:33.900 --> 00:31:34.733
or understand.

00:31:34.733 --> 00:31:37.320
And I think that is because
we had a proper understanding

00:31:37.320 --> 00:31:39.510
of what the constraints
were in that system.

00:31:39.510 --> 00:31:40.860
So I think we have an understanding

00:31:40.860 --> 00:31:43.170
of kind of what the way that
people were interacting,

00:31:43.170 --> 00:31:44.740
and we understand what
the constraints are.

00:31:44.740 --> 00:31:47.520
I think we have a lot
of successful iterations

00:31:47.520 --> 00:31:49.080
where we've predicted the trajectory

00:31:49.080 --> 00:31:50.820
of epidemics quite well.

00:31:50.820 --> 00:31:53.100
So it's not a hopeless task.

00:31:53.100 --> 00:31:55.820
It is just one where our
expectations need to be modified

00:31:55.820 --> 00:31:58.650
by our understanding of
what the constraints are.

00:31:58.650 --> 00:32:00.060
That's what I would say.

00:32:00.060 --> 00:32:02.850
- I mean, and I guess it also behooves

00:32:02.850 --> 00:32:06.060
like continual updating of predictions.

00:32:06.060 --> 00:32:07.560
So it's not even made a prediction,

00:32:07.560 --> 00:32:08.393
it's out there.

00:32:08.393 --> 00:32:10.590
It's a very live process.

00:32:10.590 --> 00:32:11.740
Eduardo and then Junal.

00:32:12.870 --> 00:32:13.703
- Thank you, Brandon.

00:32:13.703 --> 00:32:15.960
I think I'm gonna follow
up on the conversation

00:32:15.960 --> 00:32:17.310
we had yesterday overnight.

00:32:18.320 --> 00:32:20.340
What makes me think a lot of,

00:32:20.340 --> 00:32:21.630
we keep talking about understanding,

00:32:21.630 --> 00:32:23.220
we keep talking about knowledge.

00:32:23.220 --> 00:32:26.160
And when I hear colleagues like you

00:32:26.160 --> 00:32:28.560
and other colleagues who are
much more at the boundaries

00:32:28.560 --> 00:32:32.640
between applied and very
translational science,

00:32:32.640 --> 00:32:34.440
more and more I'm asking myself,

00:32:34.440 --> 00:32:37.380
who needs to understand,
who needs to know?

00:32:37.380 --> 00:32:40.050
Because at one level, I
feel like what are the time

00:32:40.050 --> 00:32:42.450
where we feel like every
citizen has to understand

00:32:42.450 --> 00:32:44.010
how the vaccine works?

00:32:44.010 --> 00:32:46.350
Are we gonna expect to
understand how that bridge

00:32:46.350 --> 00:32:49.350
that I'm gonna cross with a car was built?

00:32:49.350 --> 00:32:54.350
And so I think it's creating
a lot of social issues,

00:32:55.680 --> 00:33:00.153
confusion, problems that have
really, I mean, affect us all.

00:33:01.470 --> 00:33:02.670
What are your thoughts on do,

00:33:02.670 --> 00:33:06.720
I mean, it's thinkable
that at so many levels,

00:33:06.720 --> 00:33:08.360
we don't need everyone to understand.

00:33:08.360 --> 00:33:12.450
I mean, it used to be that
I would go to the doctor.

00:33:12.450 --> 00:33:15.360
I mean, you did a pregnancy test.

00:33:15.360 --> 00:33:17.760
You were not asking
how does the urine work

00:33:17.760 --> 00:33:18.840
with the strip, et cetera.

00:33:18.840 --> 00:33:20.990
You assume you were
pregnant, you moved on.

00:33:21.920 --> 00:33:24.930
And my worries is that,
again, in the talking

00:33:24.930 --> 00:33:27.690
and being those of us, some of us push

00:33:27.690 --> 00:33:29.580
to do more and more outreach

00:33:29.580 --> 00:33:32.310
and it's getting all very confusing

00:33:32.310 --> 00:33:33.720
and people don't know they're listening

00:33:33.720 --> 00:33:36.960
to the peer reviewed, to the peer review

00:33:36.960 --> 00:33:38.550
or a version of the paper.

00:33:38.550 --> 00:33:40.080
Either listen to the scientist,

00:33:40.080 --> 00:33:43.320
either listen to the scientist
educator slash citizen.

00:33:43.320 --> 00:33:46.410
And I really think that
there's some positive aspects

00:33:46.410 --> 00:33:48.540
to the science being siloed.

00:33:48.540 --> 00:33:50.787
We can, everyone feels
like they need to know

00:33:50.787 --> 00:33:54.600
and understand, but not
everyone has spent 30 years

00:33:54.600 --> 00:33:56.253
looking at disease transmission.

00:33:57.180 --> 00:34:02.180
- Yeah, I mean, my opinion
is, that's a tough one.

00:34:03.030 --> 00:34:05.160
So things are easier when they're siloed

00:34:05.160 --> 00:34:07.860
and less democratic in that way.

00:34:07.860 --> 00:34:11.400
When there's less input
from the public, you know?

00:34:11.400 --> 00:34:12.700
- Well, at least there's a partition

00:34:12.700 --> 00:34:14.940
between experts and non-experts.

00:34:14.940 --> 00:34:16.350
- Yeah, when there's a partition between,

00:34:16.350 --> 00:34:18.810
I mean, it's easier, we
don't have to answer.

00:34:18.810 --> 00:34:20.940
My job would be easier if I did not feel

00:34:20.940 --> 00:34:22.680
like I was compelled to communicate it

00:34:22.680 --> 00:34:24.003
with my grandmother.

00:34:25.080 --> 00:34:26.910
I would just be able to do the science

00:34:26.910 --> 00:34:28.740
and talk to you and talk to you.

00:34:28.740 --> 00:34:30.270
Unfortunately, that's not how I think,

00:34:30.270 --> 00:34:32.073
that's not where I think my job is.

00:34:33.210 --> 00:34:34.860
So I don't agree with that.

00:34:34.860 --> 00:34:38.040
I'm actually deeply
pro-democratization of science.

00:34:38.040 --> 00:34:41.280
I want a future where citizens
can actually participate

00:34:41.280 --> 00:34:42.540
in the scientific process.

00:34:42.540 --> 00:34:45.750
Now, we're a long way from
that, that's utopian, perhaps.

00:34:45.750 --> 00:34:47.850
- No, but also highly problematic.

00:34:47.850 --> 00:34:51.270
Because the sources of
getting information now

00:34:51.270 --> 00:34:53.610
and disinformation and misinformation.

00:34:53.610 --> 00:34:56.850
- But it's problematic now, even within.

00:34:56.850 --> 00:34:59.040
It's not like we're doing the job well

00:34:59.040 --> 00:35:00.240
as professional scientists.

00:35:00.240 --> 00:35:03.573
There's misinformation now
within professional science.

00:35:04.410 --> 00:35:06.930
There's gatekeeping within
professional science.

00:35:06.930 --> 00:35:09.060
There's discrimination
within professional science.

00:35:09.060 --> 00:35:10.800
So I think we need to
stop looking at ourselves

00:35:10.800 --> 00:35:12.810
as a paradigm for the way that it works,

00:35:12.810 --> 00:35:13.770
'cause it doesn't.

00:35:13.770 --> 00:35:18.420
It works for us, and we're doing fine.

00:35:18.420 --> 00:35:19.800
But I know a lot of people that aren't.

00:35:19.800 --> 00:35:21.300
- But the way we think of democracy

00:35:21.300 --> 00:35:24.660
is not having 300 million
people voting 24/7

00:35:24.660 --> 00:35:26.040
for every single thing that we do.

00:35:26.040 --> 00:35:28.290
I mean, that's a referendum,
it's not a democracy.

00:35:28.290 --> 00:35:32.070
In a democracy, I used
to defer to my doctor

00:35:32.070 --> 00:35:35.190
who, in some way, I decided he knows.

00:35:35.190 --> 00:35:38.430
And my dad told me, "You
ride the bike this way."

00:35:38.430 --> 00:35:41.370
I didn't decide to go
figure it out myself.

00:35:41.370 --> 00:35:43.620
So there has to be some
trust in those people.

00:35:43.620 --> 00:35:46.500
- But my mother has faced discrimination

00:35:46.500 --> 00:35:49.740
in the medical community often.

00:35:49.740 --> 00:35:51.720
And if she did not have me there

00:35:51.720 --> 00:35:54.840
to ask questions of
the experts growing up,

00:35:54.840 --> 00:35:57.120
she would have had a
shorter life, frankly.

00:35:57.120 --> 00:35:58.470
So that's my point.

00:35:58.470 --> 00:36:00.390
Yeah, it's easier for
me to trust the person

00:36:00.390 --> 00:36:02.970
with the expertise, and that
would have made it quicker,

00:36:02.970 --> 00:36:04.290
and that would have made it easier,

00:36:04.290 --> 00:36:06.120
and the physician might have felt better

00:36:06.120 --> 00:36:07.710
about not having to answer a question

00:36:07.710 --> 00:36:09.240
from a poor black woman,

00:36:09.240 --> 00:36:11.010
but the right thing to do was for him

00:36:11.010 --> 00:36:12.810
to answer the questions that she had

00:36:12.810 --> 00:36:14.790
about what the decision was being made.

00:36:14.790 --> 00:36:15.623
- Yeah, I mean-
- And of course,

00:36:15.623 --> 00:36:17.970
it's easier the other way, but it's wrong.

00:36:17.970 --> 00:36:19.320
- Yeah, no, absolutely.

00:36:19.320 --> 00:36:21.510
And I think part of the issue, really,

00:36:21.510 --> 00:36:26.510
is with the openness comes
certain kinds of risks.

00:36:26.660 --> 00:36:31.440
So when we are not yet done
finishing our understanding

00:36:31.440 --> 00:36:35.200
or analysis, and we
are sort of in process,

00:36:35.200 --> 00:36:38.280
that is now open to the public.

00:36:38.280 --> 00:36:42.510
And I think that creates
a lot of possibilities.

00:36:42.510 --> 00:36:44.910
So I think we have to think very hard

00:36:44.910 --> 00:36:49.740
about how to democratize
and how to open up

00:36:49.740 --> 00:36:54.000
the processes of science
as inference, really.

00:36:54.000 --> 00:36:56.670
I mean, 'cause that's what the
public really wants to know.

00:36:56.670 --> 00:36:58.920
How are you arriving at this conclusion

00:36:58.920 --> 00:37:01.200
when you're giving me a directive?

00:37:01.200 --> 00:37:03.300
I think Samantha put it
really nicely, right,

00:37:03.300 --> 00:37:06.420
that even if you give
the right information,

00:37:06.420 --> 00:37:09.420
decision-making is really complex.

00:37:09.420 --> 00:37:12.660
People may not actually use the,

00:37:12.660 --> 00:37:15.210
they may just go for the simplest version

00:37:15.210 --> 00:37:17.430
of the information that you can provide,

00:37:17.430 --> 00:37:19.140
which may not be fully accurate.

00:37:19.140 --> 00:37:20.940
- I agree with both of you intensely,

00:37:20.940 --> 00:37:24.660
and then also have worked on
discrimination in medicine,

00:37:24.660 --> 00:37:27.150
which will lead to new fears for everyone.

00:37:27.150 --> 00:37:28.590
Basically, if you tell your doctor things

00:37:28.590 --> 00:37:30.810
that they disagree with,
they'll like you less.

00:37:30.810 --> 00:37:31.890
So great.
- I've seen it happen.

00:37:31.890 --> 00:37:33.120
- Great news.

00:37:33.120 --> 00:37:35.460
But for me, it's more
about this expectation

00:37:35.460 --> 00:37:37.980
that we should all be
informed about everything,

00:37:37.980 --> 00:37:39.540
which is just humanly impossible.

00:37:39.540 --> 00:37:42.090
You can't read up about
every single decision

00:37:42.090 --> 00:37:44.190
you wanna make, and that
shouldn't be an expectation

00:37:44.190 --> 00:37:45.023
we create for people.

00:37:45.023 --> 00:37:47.580
- But I feel like every
time we talk about that,

00:37:47.580 --> 00:37:48.920
we turn the dial up to,

00:37:48.920 --> 00:37:50.400
everybody doesn't need to understand

00:37:50.400 --> 00:37:51.360
everything in the universe.

00:37:51.360 --> 00:37:53.640
And that's not, there's a lot of levels

00:37:53.640 --> 00:37:55.980
between where we are now and that.

00:37:55.980 --> 00:37:58.080
Like, what I learned in the vaccine era

00:37:58.080 --> 00:38:00.993
is people just wanted
to know how it worked.

00:38:01.950 --> 00:38:02.940
Once they learned how it worked,

00:38:02.940 --> 00:38:03.773
they went and took the vaccine.

00:38:03.773 --> 00:38:05.490
I get calls to this day,
I get emails to this day.

00:38:05.490 --> 00:38:06.860
Something I said on a podcast

00:38:06.860 --> 00:38:08.460
got their whole family vaccinated.

00:38:08.460 --> 00:38:10.260
'Cause I just took the time to explain,

00:38:10.260 --> 00:38:12.270
like it's not everybody
wants to have access

00:38:12.270 --> 00:38:13.920
to everything ever, and we got this,

00:38:13.920 --> 00:38:14.940
we're gonna be fighting for seats

00:38:14.940 --> 00:38:16.110
because everybody in the community

00:38:16.110 --> 00:38:17.940
who thinks they understand
what causation means,

00:38:17.940 --> 00:38:19.590
that's not the reality.

00:38:19.590 --> 00:38:21.690
And in fact, the reality that we create,

00:38:21.690 --> 00:38:25.530
the idea we've created has
created the war on science.

00:38:25.530 --> 00:38:28.860
The silos of, has actually
created the substrate

00:38:28.860 --> 00:38:30.750
for society to distrust science.

00:38:30.750 --> 00:38:33.630
So we need to completely
break that and prove that.

00:38:33.630 --> 00:38:37.240
- And the question is whether
the inferential process

00:38:37.240 --> 00:38:40.320
should be in full public view,

00:38:40.320 --> 00:38:42.270
how much of it should be in public view

00:38:42.270 --> 00:38:44.560
or whether it should be
in public view or not

00:38:44.560 --> 00:38:46.920
is a big sort of question to debate.

00:38:46.920 --> 00:38:48.210
Janelle.

00:38:48.210 --> 00:38:52.366
- To quickly, I wanna go back
to the point that Shawn made

00:38:52.366 --> 00:38:54.270
that it's a very different setting where

00:38:54.270 --> 00:38:55.660
knowledge itself is androgenous

00:38:55.660 --> 00:38:58.480
that knowledge is changed,
what knowledge you have

00:38:58.480 --> 00:39:00.750
and the way the knowledge
feeds into the system

00:39:00.750 --> 00:39:02.220
is affecting the system.

00:39:02.220 --> 00:39:04.920
That means that you can't
stabilize the system

00:39:04.920 --> 00:39:06.160
as an object of knowledge

00:39:06.160 --> 00:39:07.830
because everything that you know about

00:39:07.830 --> 00:39:09.510
is gonna change the system,

00:39:09.510 --> 00:39:11.980
very different setting
from traditional system

00:39:14.520 --> 00:39:17.070
and deeply important in exactly the realms

00:39:17.070 --> 00:39:18.600
that you're talking about.

00:39:18.600 --> 00:39:19.433
- Thank you for that.

00:39:19.433 --> 00:39:20.877
- Yeah, Tim.

00:39:20.877 --> 00:39:22.820
- This is just a short comment

00:39:22.820 --> 00:39:26.550
in terms of I understand this discussion

00:39:26.550 --> 00:39:28.050
and there's certainly a big problem

00:39:28.050 --> 00:39:31.470
with communicating science
and motivating people

00:39:31.470 --> 00:39:33.900
to behave the right way.

00:39:33.900 --> 00:39:36.540
And to say a lot of the
problem arises from within,

00:39:36.540 --> 00:39:41.540
but let's not ignore that
the fossil fuel industry

00:39:42.120 --> 00:39:46.110
undertook a billion-dollar
campaign to misinform people

00:39:46.110 --> 00:39:48.960
that the tobacco industry undertook

00:39:48.960 --> 00:39:51.120
billion-dollar campaigns
to misinform people

00:39:51.120 --> 00:39:53.820
and with the intentional effect

00:39:53.820 --> 00:39:56.010
to undermine faith in science.

00:39:56.010 --> 00:39:57.750
So I don't know that we should blame our,

00:39:57.750 --> 00:40:01.110
I mean, we have our
bits of blame, but yeah.

00:40:01.110 --> 00:40:04.710
- My question is what do we do?

00:40:04.710 --> 00:40:05.543
That's my question.

00:40:05.543 --> 00:40:06.510
- Yeah.

00:40:06.510 --> 00:40:08.790
- So is the answer that because
the fossil fuel industry

00:40:08.790 --> 00:40:10.680
flooded the industry of billions
and misinformed the public

00:40:10.680 --> 00:40:12.810
and I work on topics related to that,

00:40:12.810 --> 00:40:14.790
that I'm not culpable
to do anything about it?

00:40:14.790 --> 00:40:15.843
Is that the answer?

00:40:17.520 --> 00:40:18.680
- No.
- No.

00:40:18.680 --> 00:40:19.513
- No.

00:40:19.513 --> 00:40:20.680
You do something about it.

00:40:20.680 --> 00:40:24.150
We should be the ones going
to war with these people.

00:40:24.150 --> 00:40:26.040
This is what happened with eugenics.

00:40:26.040 --> 00:40:26.880
- Right.

00:40:26.880 --> 00:40:29.940
- I'm not, I just, the
other option is unconscious.

00:40:29.940 --> 00:40:32.310
- But I think Tim is absolutely right

00:40:32.310 --> 00:40:35.220
that they're having established playbooks.

00:40:35.220 --> 00:40:39.460
So at some level, we have
the advantage post-hoc

00:40:39.460 --> 00:40:41.880
of knowing how these misinformation

00:40:41.880 --> 00:40:45.140
and disinformation campaigns
can work and have worked

00:40:45.140 --> 00:40:47.670
and in what ways they are successful.

00:40:47.670 --> 00:40:51.690
And I think the, I mean, I
really like the various threads

00:40:51.690 --> 00:40:52.590
that have come up.

00:40:54.060 --> 00:40:56.130
It is a conundrum.

00:40:56.130 --> 00:41:01.130
If we are able to show and
reveal as expert scientists

00:41:02.220 --> 00:41:05.400
or people working with data sets

00:41:05.400 --> 00:41:09.000
and inferring from data sets,
whatever be those data sets,

00:41:09.000 --> 00:41:11.910
even if that process is fully demystified,

00:41:11.910 --> 00:41:15.750
it's unclear because you have
these other outside forces

00:41:15.750 --> 00:41:17.520
that are sort of banging at the door.

00:41:17.520 --> 00:41:21.213
So the question is, given
that is the reality,

00:41:22.440 --> 00:41:26.400
is talking about how we
relate effects and causes

00:41:26.400 --> 00:41:31.050
and how we arrive at decisions

00:41:31.050 --> 00:41:35.730
and how individuals then
incorporate that information

00:41:35.730 --> 00:41:39.460
and make decisions, it's
a very complex space.

00:41:39.460 --> 00:41:42.280
But I think we all share responsibility

00:41:42.280 --> 00:41:44.403
to do something about it.

00:41:45.980 --> 00:41:48.740
- Okay, one, two, and three.

00:41:50.183 --> 00:41:53.250
I wanna go to coffee, so I
wanna be attentive to that.

00:41:53.250 --> 00:41:54.083
- Yes, thank you.

00:41:54.083 --> 00:41:56.730
This is a fascinating conversation.

00:41:56.730 --> 00:42:00.833
And at the expense of
opening a can of worms.

00:42:01.900 --> 00:42:04.663
- Better be a small can.
- Very, very small can.

00:42:04.663 --> 00:42:07.113
Very small can called AI.

00:42:08.040 --> 00:42:11.190
And my question is,

00:42:11.190 --> 00:42:15.600
would it, if and when we have trustworthy,

00:42:15.600 --> 00:42:19.380
reliable, robust products and services,

00:42:19.380 --> 00:42:20.943
AI products and services,

00:42:21.900 --> 00:42:25.230
will that help democratize science?

00:42:25.230 --> 00:42:27.780
For example, your mother would have,

00:42:27.780 --> 00:42:32.190
instead of asked you and took
you with her to the doctors,

00:42:32.190 --> 00:42:34.290
she could have had her phone

00:42:34.290 --> 00:42:39.290
and asked, you know, an
AI specialized product.

00:42:39.640 --> 00:42:42.720
What do I do with the doctor?

00:42:42.720 --> 00:42:44.133
What do I tell the doctor?

00:42:45.810 --> 00:42:47.850
- I'll just end it here by saying,

00:42:47.850 --> 00:42:51.200
I find myself oddly pro-technology

00:42:51.200 --> 00:42:52.920
in a lot of these conversations,

00:42:52.920 --> 00:42:54.070
relative to my friends.

00:42:55.260 --> 00:42:56.910
Having seen, when you've
seen disinformation

00:42:56.910 --> 00:42:58.890
happen in person, you don't,

00:42:58.890 --> 00:43:00.780
I'm just not sure that that's a,

00:43:00.780 --> 00:43:04.440
I'm not sure that the
algorithmization of it is worse.

00:43:04.440 --> 00:43:05.920
- Right, but I mean, the question is-

00:43:05.920 --> 00:43:07.650
- I'm not sure, I'm not convinced.

00:43:07.650 --> 00:43:10.150
- No, but Brandon, but if the algorithm

00:43:11.220 --> 00:43:13.620
has been arrived at with biased-

00:43:13.620 --> 00:43:16.020
- Of course, totally right.

00:43:16.020 --> 00:43:17.160
- It's gonna be no better.

00:43:17.160 --> 00:43:18.390
- No, no, no, totally right.

00:43:18.390 --> 00:43:20.490
I'm just, I just don't have
faith in people like that.

00:43:20.490 --> 00:43:23.040
- Right.
(laughing)

00:43:23.040 --> 00:43:25.020
So, I had a question there.

00:43:25.020 --> 00:43:26.053
Quick question.

00:43:26.053 --> 00:43:26.886
- [Attendee] I can bring
it up at the coffee hour.

00:43:26.886 --> 00:43:27.719
- Okay, great, okay.

